==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|anadiplosis}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|ἀναδίπλωσις}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|anadiploses}}

# {{context|rhetoric|lang=en}} A [[figure of speech]] in which a [[word]] or [[phrase]] [[use]]d at the [[end]] of a [[clause]] or [[expression]] is [[repeated]] [[near]] the [[beginning]] of the [[next]] [[clause]] or [[expression]].
{{examples-right|examples="{{nowrap|Suffering produces endurance, and}} {{nowrap|endurance produces character,}} and character produces hope."<br>(Romans 5:3-4)}}

====Usage note====
Frequently combined with (but distinct from) [[climax]], so that each step of the anadiplosis typically increases in magnitude or rhetorical force, with the effect of making the last term more powerful by comparison.

